    <h2>The Myth That LVP Is Always the Answer</h2>

    <p>Luxury vinyl plank earned its popularity honestly — it's waterproof, tough, and forgiving, and for a great many floors it's the right pick. The myth is the "always." Sold as the universal answer, it gets installed over floors that never should have been covered and in situations where a different material would serve better. The one-size-fits-all pitch is easy to sell because it stops the conversation, and stopping the conversation is exactly what costs a homeowner the better outcome.</p>

    <h2>The Hardwood Under Your Old Floor Is Often Worth Saving</h2>

    <p>Here's the reality the LVP pitch skips: many older Florence and Sheffield homes were built with solid hardwood — often 3/4-inch oak or pine — that's still there under carpet, old vinyl, or a tired finish. Solid hardwood can be sanded and refinished several times over its life, and a refinish frequently costs less than a new floor while restoring something no manufactured plank can imitate. Pulling up carpet in a 1930s home and finding original hardwood is a good day; covering that same floor with LVP because someone said it's easier is a genuine loss. A flooring contractor worth hiring checks what's underneath before recommending anything.</p>

    <h2>What Good Installers Check Before They Start</h2>

    <p>The difference between a floor that lasts and a callback is the prep nobody photographs. A careful installer meters the moisture in the slab or subfloor, confirms the subfloor is flat within tolerance, and lets the new flooring acclimate in the home for a few days so it's at the house's humidity before it's locked down. On a refinish, they check the remaining thickness of the hardwood to be sure there's enough wood left to sand. These steps are unglamorous and they're where the corner gets cut — the honest truth is that a fast crew skipping acclimation and moisture testing in a humid climate is setting up the cupping they'll blame on the product later.</p>

    <h2>Moisture Is the Shoals Wildcard</h2>

    <p>The region's humidity, and damp slabs in ground-contact rooms, make moisture the single biggest threat to a new floor here. Wood and moisture-sensitive products swell and cup when they take on water from below, and a slab that meters wet needs a vapor barrier or a mitigation step before anything goes over it. This is where LVP genuinely earns its keep — in a below-grade room or over a slab where solid hardwood would be a mistake. Matching the material to the moisture reality of the specific room, rather than defaulting to one product everywhere, is the whole game.</p>

    <h2>Red Flags in a Flooring Bid</h2>

    <p>A bid to install over an existing floor without anyone checking what's underneath is skipping the most valuable question. An installer who won't meter subfloor moisture in a humid climate, or who dismisses acclimation as unnecessary, is inviting the cupping callback. And a quote that recommends the same product for a dry upstairs bedroom and a damp ground-floor room hasn't actually looked at the house.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Flooring Costs Across the Shoals</h2>

    <p>Real ranges, and what moves them:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Luxury vinyl plank</strong> — roughly $3 to $7 per square foot installed.</li>
      <li><strong>Refinishing existing hardwood</strong> — about $3.50 to $8 per square foot, often cheaper than replacement.</li>
      <li><strong>New hardwood or tile</strong> — $8 to $15-plus per square foot installed, by material.</li>
      <li><strong>Subfloor repair or moisture mitigation</strong> — a separate line, and worth doing before the finish floor goes down.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The newer Muscle Shoals builds lean toward straightforward LVP installs; the older Florence and Sheffield homes are where refinishing original hardwood becomes the higher-value option.</p>

    <h2>Timing a Floor the Right Way</h2>

    <p>The moment to act on a hardwood floor is before the finish wears through to bare wood, because a floor caught in time gets refinished while one worn to the wood may need boards replaced first. A new-construction or remodel floor should go in after the wet trades and the drywall dust are done, not before. And any room that has ever taken on water deserves a moisture check before new flooring, so you're not sealing a problem under a fresh floor.</p>

